Sorry that these messages didn't get through. Apparently, they were marked as spam for some reason. Copying here the hints I gave you privately for more visibility:
mclapply and family don't work on Windows. The simmer environment (like any other external pointer) is not serializable, and therefore cannot be shared in any other parallelization method. If you have access to a Linux machine, you can parallelize very efficiently via mclapply, by building the environment once and then sharing it. But on Windows you need to build the environment at least once in every worker (then you can simulate more than once per worker with the same environment).
Whenever you parallelize, but also when you replicate via lapply/purrr tools by resetting and reusing the environment, you need to wrap() the final result. In a parallel run, this means that the results of your simulation can be exported back to the main process. In serial lapply/purrr runs, this means that you get a list of all the different simulation results, and not a list pointing to the same simulation environment, which only holds the results for your last simulation.
Hope it helps.
Iñaki

